STEP-04 — REPOSITORY ALIGNMENT

Purpose: Before creating a single interface or writing code, align the Capability Environment with the existing COREI repositories to prevent duplicate frameworks, managers, registries, runtimes, and contracts.

1. Alignment Philosophy

COREI is not a greenfield project.

The Capability Environment shall:

Extend existing platform architecture.
Reuse existing runtime infrastructure.
Consume existing institutional services.
Preserve architectural consistency.
Avoid introducing parallel frameworks.

The default decision is:

Reuse
    ↓
Extend
    ↓
Compose
    ↓
Create (only if necessary)

Creation is the last option.

2. Repository Alignment Matrix

Before implementing any component, every artifact shall be classified.

Decision	Meaning
Reuse	Existing implementation satisfies requirement.
Extend	Existing implementation is enhanced.
Compose	New component orchestrates existing components.
Replace	Existing implementation is retired through approved migration.
Create	No suitable implementation exists.

Every file shall have exactly one alignment decision.

3. Capability Manager Alignment

Questions to answer:

Does a generic Manager Framework already exist?
Does Runtime already coordinate managers?
Does Operating Environment already expose lifecycle managers?
Does Operator Environment already expose participation managers?

Decision process:

Existing Manager
        │
        ├── Suitable
        │        ↓
        │      Extend
        │
        ├── Partially Suitable
        │        ↓
        │     Compose
        │
        └── Missing
                 ↓
              Create

No duplicate manager hierarchy.

4. Registry Alignment

Review existing registries.

Examples:

Workspace Registry
Experience Registry
Component Registry
Service Registry
Runtime Registry
Domain Registry

Questions:

Can Capability Registry inherit registry infrastructure?
Can metadata format be reused?
Can lookup mechanism be shared?

Registry implementation shall follow one institutional pattern.

5. Runtime Alignment

Review existing runtime.

Already defined:

Operating Runtime
Workspace Runtime
Experience Runtime
Session Runtime

Capability Runtime shall determine:

What is inherited?
What is composed?
What is capability-specific?

Capability Runtime shall never become another platform runtime.

6. Lifecycle Alignment

Review lifecycle already defined.

Examples:

Operating Lifecycle
Workspace Lifecycle
Session Lifecycle
Operator Lifecycle

Capability Lifecycle shall reuse:

lifecycle state machine
transition validation
event publication
recovery mechanisms

Only capability-specific transitions may be added.

7. Contract Alignment

Review existing contracts.

Examples:

Workspace Contract
Experience Contract
Runtime Contract
Component Contract
Service Contract

Capability Contracts shall:

follow existing contract conventions
reuse shared interfaces
preserve naming consistency

No competing contract styles.

8. State Alignment

Review existing state framework.

Questions:

Is there already an authoritative state engine?
Are Zustand stores already standardized?
Are runtime states already modeled?

Capability state shall integrate with the institutional state model.

9. Event Alignment

Review existing event infrastructure.

Examples:

Kafka events
Internal runtime events
UI events
Domain events

Capability events shall integrate with existing event infrastructure.

No parallel event bus.

10. Service Alignment

Review shared platform services.

Examples:

Configuration
Logging
Metrics
Tracing
Validation
Discovery

Capability Environment shall consume these services rather than implement replacements.

11. Security Alignment

Review Operating Environment.

Already owns:

Authentication
Authorization
Identity
Permissions
Policies

Capability Environment shall consume security.

It shall never own security.

12. Telemetry Alignment

Review existing observability.

Already implemented:

OpenTelemetry
Prometheus
Loki
Jaeger

Capability telemetry shall publish through existing observability infrastructure.

13. Configuration Alignment

Review configuration framework.

Questions:

Is configuration centralized?
Is environment loading standardized?
Are feature flags already implemented?

Capability configuration shall reuse existing configuration services.

14. Repository Alignment Checklist

Every implementation artifact must answer the following before creation.

Question	Required
Does this already exist?	✔
Which repository owns it?	✔
Can it be reused?	✔
Can it be extended?	✔
Can it be composed?	✔
Does it introduce duplication?	Must be No
Does it preserve architecture?	✔
Does it follow existing contracts?	✔

If any answer is unresolved, implementation pauses until alignment is complete.

15. Expected Alignment Output

At the end of this step, every planned file from STEP-03 will have an explicit implementation decision.

Example:

Planned Component	Existing Owner	Decision	Action
Capability Manager	Existing Manager Framework	Extend	Add capability coordination
Capability Registry	Registry Framework	Extend	Register capability metadata
Capability Runtime	Operating Runtime	Compose	Create capability execution layer
Capability Contracts	Shared Contracts	Extend	Add capability-specific interfaces
Capability Events	Existing Event System	Reuse	Define new event types only
Capability Telemetry	Observability Framework	Reuse	Publish capability metrics
16. Completion Criteria

STEP-04 is complete when:

Every planned file has been mapped to an existing COREI component or explicitly justified as new.
No duplicate frameworks remain in the design.
Ownership boundaries are confirmed.
Reuse, extension, and composition decisions are frozen.
The Capability Environment is fully aligned with the existing COREI architecture.
Next Step

Only after this alignment is complete do we proceed to STEP-05 — Interface & Contract Design, where every file receives a concrete TypeScript interface and public API based on the existing COREI architecture rather than on newly invented abstractions. This ensures the implementation evolves the platform instead of creating parallel systems.

